  "original_smiles": "Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1",
  "original_smiles_valid": true,
  "original_canonical_smiles": "Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1",
  "disease_target": "HIV-1 Protease",
  "mechanism": "Competitive peptidomimetic inhibitor",
  "clinical_context": "HIV antiretroviral (now largely replaced by newer agents)",
  "user_prompt": "Suggest 3 structural modifications to Lopinavir (SMILES: Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1) to improve its pharmacological profile against HIV-1 Protease. For each modification, provide:\n1. A description of the change\n2. The modified SMILES string\n3. Scientific rationale\n\nFormat each modification clearly and include the SMILES on its own line.",
  "raw_response": "### **Modification 1: Introduction of a fluorine atom on the aromatic ring**\n1. **Description**: Add a fluorine atom to the phenyl ring of the P1\u2019 side chain to improve binding affinity and metabolic stability. Fluorine can enhance hydrophobic interactions with the HIV-1 protease active site and increase resistance to enzymatic degradation.\n2. **Modified SMILES**:  \n   `Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2F)[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1`\n3. **Scientific Rationale**: Fluorination often improves the metabolic stability of a drug by making the compound more resistant to oxidative metabolism. Additionally, fluorine's electron-withdrawing properties can enhance binding interactions with the protease active site by modulating the electronic properties of the molecule.\n\n---\n\n### **Modification 2: Replace the phenyl ring in the P2 position with a cyclopropyl group**\n1. **Description**: Replace the phenyl ring in the P2 side chain with a cyclopropyl group to improve hydrophobic binding and reduce potential steric clashes in the protease active site.\n2. **Modified SMILES**:  \n   `Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)[C@@H](O)C[C@@H](CC1)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1`\n3. **Scientific Rationale**: Cyclopropyl groups are compact and highly hydrophobic, which can increase binding affinity in hydrophobic pockets of the protease active site. This substitution may also reduce steric hindrance and improve drug-protein interactions while potentially reducing off-target interactions.\n\n---\n\n### **Modification 3: Methylation of the secondary alcohol**\n1. **Description**: Replace the secondary alcohol (-OH) group with a methoxy (-OCH3) group to improve oral bioavailability and reduce susceptibility to glucuronidation.\n2. **Modified SMILES**:  \n   `Cc1ccc(C(=O)N[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)[C@@H](OC)C[C@@H](Cc2ccccc2)NC(=O)c2nc3ccccc3n2C)nc1`\n3. **Scientific Rationale**: By replacing the hydroxyl group with a methoxy group, the molecule becomes less polar, which can improve membrane permeability and oral bioavailability. This modification may also reduce the rate of glucuronidation, thus improving metabolic stability and increasing the drug's half-life.\n\n---\n\nThese modifications aim to improve the binding affinity, metabolic stability, and pharmacokinetic properties of Lopinavir while maintaining or enhancing its activity against HIV-1 protease.",
